        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive, idiomatic extraction:\n\n- The new length check lives in a well-named private helper (`nationalLengthValid`) in the same class that already owns E.164 parsing \u2014 the right unit for this logic. It isn't shoved inline into an already-dense return expression, and it doesn't leak into a new catch-all class.\n- `toE164` keeps a flat early-return shape (`matches` check, then length check, then build), which reads naturally and matches the \"validate, then produce\" flow the method already had.\n- Each distinct validation concern is its own guard rather than one compound condition, so a maintainer can see at a glance what each rejection means.\n- The Javadoc on both the public method and the helper was updated to describe the new behavior, consistent with the existing documentation style in this file.\n\nThe one thing I considered flagging is that country-code knowledge is now expressed in two places \u2014 `toE164` builds the `\"61\"` prefix, and `nationalLengthValid` re-identifies country by `startsWith`/offset \u2014 but for two hard-coded country codes this is proportionate and not worth introducing an abstraction over. No structural changes needed."
